 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 54,
   "plaque": "ascension_green_mountain",
   "title": "Green Mountain",
   "text": "From the sea Ascension looks like a bare cinder heap, yet its highest peak, at 859 metres, wears a cloud forest. In 1843 Joseph Hooker, a botanist, visited and advised on making the mountain wet. The Navy shipped in thousands of plants from Kew, Argentina, South Africa and the Cape, and the rain followed."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 40,
   "plaque": "ascension_hooker",
   "title": "Hooker's Idea",
   "text": "Joseph Hooker, of Kew, thought that if trees and ferns caught enough cloud on the top of the mountain, they would drip water into the soil and keep the island in water. Charles Darwin supported the idea. A great many species were planted over the next decades, and the mountain is now a patchwork of bamboo, ginger, Norfolk pine and tree ferns from half the world."
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 10,
   "plaque": "ascension_dampier",
   "title": "Dampier's Drip",
   "text": "In 1701 the explorer William Dampier's ship, HMS Roebuck, sank at Ascension, and he and his crew survived on a trickle of fresh water on the mountain. It is still called Dampier's Drip. Until the Marines came, it was one of the few places on the island where drinking water could be found."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 41,
   "plaque": "ascension_farm",
   "title": "The Farm",
   "text": "The Royal Marines built terraces, walls and a pipeline here in the 1800s, so that the garrison could grow vegetables and keep a few cattle and pigs. Everything was carried up and down by hand or by donkey. It was the Navy's way of saying 'we are staying'."
  },
  {
   "x": 16,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "ascension_devils_riding",
   "title": "The Devil's Riding School",
   "text": "A flat-bottomed crater on the south-east coast, down at Comfortless Cove, where sand and dust blow so smoothly that it looks like an arena. The sailors named it. Standing there on a hot day, it is not hard to believe somebody once practised riding a very large horse."
  },
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 24,
   "plaque": "ascension_bamboo",
   "title": "Bamboo on the Mountain",
   "text": "Look about you: the tall green canes are bamboo, introduced as a windbreak and a building material. It now grows in thick stands near the summit. Nothing here is native except the ferns that grow on the highest rocks, which were already here when the people arrived."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 38,
   "plaque": "ascension_wideawake_fair",
   "title": "The Wideawake Fair",
   "text": "Sooty terns, nicknamed wideawakes for their constant calling, nest in the open on the island's plains. Before the rats and cats were cleared the colonies were tiny. The numbers are climbing again, and the 'fair' on the plain is one of the great noisy gatherings of the Atlantic."
  }
 ],
